<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- OstrichChallenge: REPAIRING BROKEN VENDING MACHINES

     Dukes are the hotel's only money and the vending machines are the only shop, so a machine
     that spawned broken is a dead end. Vanilla's only answer is to wrench it for scrap. This
     lets the player put one back into service instead: wrench the dead ones, build a kit at a
     workbench, and revive the machine you want.

     WHY THIS IS A BLOCK UPGRADE AND NOT SOMETHING CLEVERER
     The working machine is, in vanilla's own words, the broken one plus a class: cntVendingMachine2
     carries Extends="cntVendingMachine2Broken" and adds only Class="VendingMachine", a model and a
     TraderID. Same footprint, same material, same MultiBlockDim. So the repair is exactly what
     UpgradeBlock is for, and it is a stock mechanism used in seventy places in vanilla blocks.xml.

     WHICH MACHINES QUALIFY
     Only the ones that cannot trade at all, which is the ones with no Class property. That is the
     three drinks machines and the EMPTY snack machine. cntVendingMachineSnacksFullBroken keeps
     Class="VendingMachine" and TraderID 10, so it already trades despite looking wrecked, and it is
     deliberately left alone. Counter-intuitive but vanilla.

     WHY EACH BLOCK GETS ITS OWN COPY RATHER THAN ONE ON THE PARENT
     The energy, water and snack variants all Extend cntVendingMachine2Broken. Putting UpgradeBlock
     only on the parent would make every one of them repair into a SODA machine. Each therefore
     declares its own, pointing at its own working counterpart.
     >>> WORTH ONE CHECK IN GAME: a child's own class-property should override the inherited one.
     If a repaired water machine comes back as a soda machine, that assumption is wrong and the fix
     is to stop putting UpgradeBlock on the parent and give the soda machine its own derived block.

     NOT DELIVERED HERE, AND IT MATTERS
     A repaired machine will be FULLY STOCKED the moment it is first opened, not empty. Trader stock
     resets on lastInventoryUpdate + ResetIntervalInTicks, and a newly created machine has that
     timestamp at zero, so its next reset is already long past. Making it empty on repair and
     stocking later needs the DLL to stamp the timestamp and clear the inventory when the upgrade
     completes. Until that exists, this file alone does NOT give the "no loot when repaired"
     behaviour that was asked for.
-->
<configs>

    <!-- ============================================================
         REPAIR COST: MECHANICAL PARTS, NOT FORGED STEEL

         A block has to be at full health before it will upgrade, so a machine that spawned
         damaged has to be patched up before the kit will go in. Vanilla charges 10 forged steel
         for that, which is the wrong currency: you are fixing a machine, not a wall.

         NOTE ON REACH. Only cntVendingMachine2Broken declares RepairItems. Every other drinks
         and snacks machine extends it, INCLUDING THE WORKING ONES, so this single change sets
         the repair cost for all eleven of them, not just the four we make repairable. That is
         consistent rather than accidental, but it is wider than the four blocks below.
         cntVendingMachine, the player-placed one, declares its own and is untouched.

         6 rather than 10 because the kit already costs 10 mechanical parts and patching dents
         should not rival building the kit. This is the full-damage price; partial damage costs
         proportionally less.
         ============================================================ -->
    <remove xpath="//block[@name='cntVendingMachine2Broken']/property[@class='RepairItems']"/>
    <append xpath="//block[@name='cntVendingMachine2Broken']">
        <property class="RepairItems">
            <property name="resourceMechanicalParts" value="6"/>
        </property>
    </append>

    <!-- Soda -->
    <append xpath="//block[@name='cntVendingMachine2Broken']">
        <property class="UpgradeBlock">
            <property name="ToBlock" value="cntVendingMachine2"/>
            <property name="Item" value="occVendingRepairKit"/>
            <property name="ItemCount" value="1"/>
            <property name="UpgradeHitCount" value="4"/>
        </property>
    </append>

    <!-- Energy -->
    <append xpath="//block[@name='cntVendingMachineEnergyBroken']">
        <property class="UpgradeBlock">
            <property name="ToBlock" value="cntVendingMachineEnergy"/>
            <property name="Item" value="occVendingRepairKit"/>
            <property name="ItemCount" value="1"/>
            <property name="UpgradeHitCount" value="4"/>
        </property>
    </append>

    <!-- Water -->
    <append xpath="//block[@name='cntVendingMachineWaterBroken']">
        <property class="UpgradeBlock">
            <property name="ToBlock" value="cntVendingMachineWater"/>
            <property name="Item" value="occVendingRepairKit"/>
            <property name="ItemCount" value="1"/>
            <property name="UpgradeHitCount" value="4"/>
        </property>
    </append>

    <!-- ============================================================
         ROOM SAFES POINT AT THE FIRST-CRACK LIST

         An unlocked safe is where the loot actually is: the locked block is never opened, because
         picking it downgrades it to this one and breaking it produces the same block. So this is
         the only list that matters, and it starts rich. The DLL repoints it at occSafeRestock once
         the safe has been opened, and lootListName persists with the save.

         ONE CHANGE COVERS DESK AND WALL SAFES. cntDeskSafeInsecure extends cntWallSafeInsecure and
         excludes only HandleFace, so it inherits these features. cntWallSafeOpen also extends it
         but excludes CompositeFeatures entirely, so the emptied husk is untouched and stays empty.

         GUN SAFES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT INCLUDED. They are a separate chain on the gunSafe list and
         are not what goes in a hotel room. Say if the hotel has any and they should behave the same.
         ============================================================ -->
    <set xpath="//block[@name='cntWallSafeInsecure']/property[@class='CompositeFeatures']/property[@class='TEFeatureStorage']/property[@name='LootList']/@value">occSafeFirst</set>

    <!-- ============================================================
         PICKING A SAFE HAS TO BE WORTH DOING

         Vanilla sets LockPickBreakChance to 0.75, and TEFeatureLockPickable turns that into

             picks needed = floor( ln(1 - nth) / ln(breakChance) ) + 1,  capped at 15

         with nth rolled per safe. At 0.75 that averages FOUR lockpicks to open one safe, with a
         tail into nine or more. A first crack returns one pick at 10%, so picking was running at
         roughly forty times net negative and no player who noticed would ever do it. The pick
         route existed but was a trap.

         At 0.25 the median is one pick, the worst quarter is two, and the average is about 1.3.
         Picking still costs more than it returns, which is right, picks should be a consumable
         rather than self-sustaining. But the choice becomes real: quiet and costs you a pick, or
         loud and free and brings the floor down on you.

         LockPickTime is left at 15 seconds on purpose. Standing still for a quarter of a minute
         in a hotel full of guests is the other half of the price, and it is the interesting half.

         One change covers both safes: cntDeskSafe extends cntWallSafe.
         Perks still apply on top, EffectManager scales both the time and the break chance.
         ============================================================ -->
    <set xpath="//block[@name='cntWallSafe']/property[@class='CompositeFeatures']/property[@class='TEFeatureLockPickable']/property[@name='LockPickBreakChance']/@value">0.25</set>

    <!-- Snacks, empty. The FULL broken one still trades and is left alone on purpose. -->
    <append xpath="//block[@name='cntVendingMachineSnacksEmptyBroken']">
        <property class="UpgradeBlock">
            <property name="ToBlock" value="cntVendingMachineSnacksFull"/>
            <property name="Item" value="occVendingRepairKit"/>
            <property name="ItemCount" value="1"/>
            <property name="UpgradeHitCount" value="4"/>
        </property>
    </append>

    <!-- ============================================================
         FIBRE IN THE POT PLANTS (v1.5.2)

         A concrete tower has no grass, so the hotel contains no plant fibre worth speaking of and
         the opening hour stalls: you cannot make the most basic tool. Fril, after the first long
         run: "I also barely found any stones or fibre so making a basic stone axe at the start was
         hard."

         WHY treePlant02 AND NOTHING ELSE. It is the base class for every potted plant in the
         building. All thirteen of these carry Extends="treePlant02" (directly or one step up) and
         all thirteen are in hotel_02's block list:

             plantHouseTallPottedBlack / Brown / Grey / White
             plantHousePottedRed, plantHousePottedCornerBlack
             plantFernBlack, plantFernHangingBrown / White
             plantCherryBlossomBlue / Red / White
             treePlant02 itself

         Drop inheritance is not an assumption here. BlocksFromXml.LoadExtendedItemDrops calls
         Block.CopyDroppedFrom(parent), which walks the parent's drops per event and adds each one
         the child does not already declare BY ITEM NAME. None of the thirteen declares a fibre
         drop, so one edit reaches all of them and none of them can block it.

         BOTH EVENTS, and this is the part my first draft got wrong. treePlant02 ships with a
         Destroy drop (6 clay lumps) and no Harvest drop at all. Destroy alone would have missed
         every break made with a tool. This is Fril's pattern from Area 7 and the Hospital, both
         shipped and both working: list the drop under both events, and whichever one the break
         resolves to pays out.

         THE TRAP, worth recording because it nearly cost a session. The item is resourceYuccaFibers
         and there is no resourcePlantFibers in 3.x, only the one fibre item in the whole game. The
         hotel also contains plantedYucca3Harvest, which looks like the obvious source and is not:
         it drops foodCropYuccaFruit, and its parent cropsHarvestableMaster carries a
         resourceYuccaFibers line with count="0". Harvesting the hotel's yucca gives fruit and
         nothing else. Before this change the ONLY fibre in hotel_02 was plantHangingIvy01 and
         plantHangingIvy02, at 2-4 each on Destroy.

         THE RATE, CHANGED IN v1.13.1 AND MY EARLIER REASONING WAS WRONG.

         v1.5.2 shipped 10-15 at prob 0.1, copying Area 7, and this comment argued for it: "a
         rare handful is a moment and a reliable one or two is a chore." Fril has now played the
         hotel and asked for 2-6 instead, reliably.

         That is not a small change. 10-15 at one in ten averages 1.25 fibres per plant; 2-6
         always averages 4. THE HOTEL NOW YIELDS 3.2x THE FIBRE IT DID, and the feel changes
         from a lottery to a supply. Recorded plainly because it reverses a deliberate decision
         rather than tuning a number: the argument for rarity was made before anyone had played
         a full opening hour in a concrete tower, and play beat it.

         NOT MEASURED: how many plants a floor actually holds. hotel_02_blocks.nim is a name index,
         so it proves the thirteen types are present and says nothing about counts. If this reads
         thin or overflowing in play, that count is the thing to get before touching the number.
         ============================================================ -->
    <append xpath="/blocks/block[@name='treePlant02']">
        <drop event="Harvest" name="resourceYuccaFibers" count="2,6"/>
        <drop event="Destroy" name="resourceYuccaFibers" count="2,6"/>
    </append>

    <!-- ============================================================
         FIBRE FROM SCRAPPING THE POT PLANTS TOO (v1.13.2)

         Fril: "When I scrap primitive armor in my inventory it gives me back fibre. Id like the
         same thing to happen with treePlant02."

         HOW SCRAPPING ACTUALLY DECIDES WHAT YOU GET, read out of the IL rather than guessed,
         because the obvious answer is wrong. A thing does NOT scrap into its recipe's
         ingredients. CraftingManager.GetScrapableRecipe looks at ONE thing:

             itemValue.ItemClass.MadeOfMaterial.ForgeCategory

         It then walks every recipe tagged salvageScrap (the seventeen <wildcard_forge_category/>
         recipes at the top of vanilla recipes.xml) and returns the one whose OUTPUT item has the
         same forge_category. One category in, one resource out. Nothing else is consulted, and
         an item can only ever scrap into a single resource.

         So primitive armor gives fibre for exactly one reason: armorPrimitiveMaster carries
         Material="Mplants", and Mplants has forge_category="plants", and the salvageScrap recipe
         whose output sits in "plants" is resourceYuccaFibers. That is the whole mechanism.

         WHAT THE PLANTS DO TODAY. Picking a potted plant up does not give you treePlant02; it
         gives you plantPottedVariantHelper (CanPickup param1). That helper Extends treePlant02,
         so it inherits Material="MfertilizedClayPlanter", whose forge_category is "clay" —
         and clay's salvageScrap output is resourceClayLump. Scrapping a picked-up plant has
         always given 6 clay lumps, which is why it looked like it did nothing interesting.

         THE CHANGE IS ONE PROPERTY, and it is deliberately on the HELPER and not on treePlant02.
         plantPottedVariantHelper is an inventory-only block: SelectAlternates + PlaceAltBlockValue
         mean placing it always resolves to one of the 80-odd real potted plants, never to the
         helper itself. So its Material governs scrapping and nothing else — no hardness, no step
         sound, no stability, because the block is never in the world. Putting Mplants on
         treePlant02 instead would have changed how every pot plant in the hotel takes damage.

         It reaches all of them anyway: every potted variant inherits CanPickup from treePlant02,
         so they all pick up into this one helper.

         THE AMOUNT, and this is the part worth knowing before anyone retunes it.
         ItemActionEntryScrap.OnActivated computes:

             out = (sourceWeight * stackCount / outputWeight) * 0.75 * ScrappingOutputModifier

         resourceYuccaFibers has Weight="1", so out = sourceWeight * 0.75 per plant. Weight="4"
         below gives 3 fibre. That is not a number I invented: ItemClass.AutoCalcWeight would
         derive exactly 4 on its own from the helper's recipe, because it sums only the
         ingredients whose forge_category matches the OUTPUT's — with Mplants on, that is
         resourceYuccaFibers count="4" and the 8 clay lumps stop counting. It is written out
         explicitly so the number is ours and survives a vanilla recipe change.

         To retune: Weight="6" gives 4 fibre, Weight="8" gives 6.

         ScrappingOutputModifier is the "ScrappingOutput" SANDBOX OPTION, so the yield moves with
         the preset. At 0.5 three fibre becomes one. Worth remembering when the presets get
         rebuilt.

         WHAT IS GIVEN UP. Scrap returns one resource, so the 6 clay lumps go. Smashing the plant
         is untouched and still pays 6 clay plus the 2-6 fibre above, so breaking remains the
         better haul and picking up stays what it is for: moving a plant, or carrying fibre home
         in one slot.

         NOT MEASURED: nothing here has been seen in play yet. The formula is read from IL and the
         forge_category chain is read from vanilla materials.xml, but the first time a plant is
         actually scrapped is the test.
         ============================================================ -->
    <append xpath="/blocks/block[@name='plantPottedVariantHelper']">
        <property name="Material" value="Mplants"/>
        <property name="Weight" value="4"/>
    </append>

    <!-- ============================================================
         THE ARUK POSTER (v1.5.2)

         Alzheimer's Research UK, for Game Over for Dementia. Carried over from the Hospital
         Challenge, same block definition.

         THE MODEL. #@modfolder resolves to THIS mod's folder, not the Hospital's, so the line
         below needs OstrichChallenge/Resources/hospitalPoster.unity3d. Fril copied the bundle
         across on 29 Sep. The alternative was #@modfolder(HospitalChallenge): to borrow the
         Hospital's copy, rejected because it would make this mod refuse to load without that one
         installed, which is not a trade worth making for one poster.

         NOTE FOR FUTURE PACKAGING: the .unity3d bundle is NOT in the source zips, because it is
         not in the build container. Resources/ has to survive being overwritten by a new drop.

         THE ICON. CustomIcon="ARUK" needs ARUK.png in UIAtlases/ItemIconAtlas, which before this
         held only occChocNAwe.png.

         Localization.csv carries posterARUKTitle and posterARUKDesc. Placeholder wording, change
         it to whatever the charity page says.
         ============================================================ -->
    <append xpath="/blocks">

        <block name="posterARUK">
            <property name="TitleKey" value="posterARUKTitle"/>
            <property name="DescriptionKey" value="posterARUKDesc"/>
            <property name="CustomIcon" value="ARUK"/>
            <property name="CreativeMode" value="Player"/>
            <property name="Material" value="Mpaper"/>
            <property name="StabilitySupport" value="false"/>
            <property name="Shape" value="ModelEntity"/>
            <property name="Model" value="#@modfolder:Resources/hospitalPoster.unity3d?ARUK.prefab"/>
            <property name="Place" value="TowardsPlacerInverted"/>
            <property name="IsDecoration" value="true"/>
            <property name="Stacknumber" value="10" />
            <property name="CanPickup" value="false"/>
            <property name="ImposterDontBlock" value="true"/>
            <drop event="Destroy" count="0"/>
            <property name="FilterTags" value="fdecor,fother"/>
            <property name="SortOrder1" value="7000"/>
            <property name="SortOrder2" value="0002"/>
            <property name="SellableToTrader" value="false"/>
            <property name="Group" value="Decor/Miscellaneous"/>
        </block>

    </append>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         HOTEL DOOR TOUGHNESS IS NOT IN THIS FILE ANY MORE (v1.72.0)

         v1.70.0 set MaxDamage to 10,000 on four door families and v1.71.0 resynced their mesh
         thresholds to match. BOTH ARE GONE, seven <set> lines removed, nothing replaces them here.

         WHY. Fril asked for "hotel doors that we sweep only, not player made doors". The second
         half was honoured; the first is NOT EXPRESSIBLE IN THIS FILE. MaxDamage is a field on
         Block, the TYPE, and BlockValue carries only accumulated damage, so there are no
         per-instance hit points and any <set> here is global to that block type in every POI in
         the world. The v1.70.0 comment that stood here KNEW that, wrote it down, and then
         reasoned past it with "it does not matter here". It did matter.

         IT WAS ALSO AIMED AT THE WRONG DOORS. Cross-referenced against The Ostrich II's own block
         list, two of the four families are NOT IN THE BUILDING AT ALL (interiorHouseDoorWhite,
         exteriorHouseDoorDoubleOldWhite), while the doors that ARE in it went untouched:

             commercialDoorV1Brown / V1Grey / V2Brown  1000
             commercialGlassDoor                        100   <- the snipe-through-the-door one
             commercialBulletproofGlassDoor            3250
             chainlinkFenceDoor                         500
             interiorDoorOldWhite                       250   (the only one v1.70.0 actually hit)
             trailerDoorBrown                           300
             elevatorDoor                             10000   (already, vanilla)
             tallCabinetDoorBlack                         1   (furniture, deliberately excluded)

         AND IT COLLIDED WITH FrilCleanDoors, which derives its mesh thresholds from the vanilla
         MaxDamage. Raising the HP made every house door in the world render boarded up again.

         IT IS ALL IN CODE NOW, in DoorSweep.cs, scoped to the tower box. MaxDamage stays vanilla
         everywhere, so FrilCleanDoors stays correct and nothing in this mod overlaps it. See
         "DOOR TOUGHNESS" in DoorSweep.cs, and the "occ doors tough" command.
         ================================================================== -->

    <!-- ==================================================================
         v1.194.0. THE PATHFINDER NEVER LOOKED AT THE BULLETPROOF GLASS.

         Fril, 22 Oct, after a test with every other mod removed: "I swapped out the
         bulletproof glass for other shapes and all the zombies still chose to break it",
         and "It has only started happening this bad wuth the diag bullet proof glass
         windows and now diag plates."

         WHAT PathType ACTUALLY DOES, read out of AstarVoxelGrid::CheckHeights rather
         than from the comment I had written about it, which was wrong:

             if (block.PathType <= 0) goto skip;                     // never even asks
             if (block.IsMovementBlocked(world, pos, bv, side)) ...  // only asked when > 0

         PathType decides whether the grid bothers to ASK about the block at all. At 0 the
         grid assumes you can walk through and never looks.

         v1.213.0 CORRECTION. The next sentence used to read "Block::IsMovementBlocked
         loops all six sides and is shape aware, so when it IS asked it gives the right
         answer." That is wrong and it was written from the method's name, not its IL.
         Block::IsMovementBlocked, read properly:

             if (!IsCollideMovement)   return false;
             if (BlocksMovement == 0)  return shape.IsMovementBlocked(...);
             return BlocksMovement == 1;

         and the shape it defers to:

             BlockShape::IsMovementBlocked  =>  GetStepHeight(...) > 0.5f
             BlockShape::GetStepHeight      =>  bv.Block.IsCollideMovement ? 1f : 0f

         There IS a six-side loop, in the overloads that take a side mask, but every side
         returns the same answer because the core overload never reads the side argument
         and never looks at the model. Only BlockShapeGrass, BlockShapeWater and
         BlockShapeBillboardCross override it, and all three are two bytes of IL returning
         false. BlockShapeNew and BlockShapeModelEntity do not override it at all.

         So for any block that collides with movement and does not set BlocksMovement,
         "asked" and "blocked on every side" are the same thing.

         So a path=0 block is a free guess, and for most shapes the guess is true: you
         really can walk over a plate or step a half block. For a DIAGONAL pane that
         fills the cell the guess is simply wrong, nothing corrects it, the route goes
         straight through the glass, and the zombie then walks into a real collider,
         wedges, and EAIBreakBlock smashes whatever it is touching. Fril's own
         observation is the tell: half-block walls and plates never caused this, the
         diagonal windows did.

         WHY vanilla LEAVES MOST BLOCKS AT 0. A collision query per cell is not free.
         TFP marked 292 blocks Path="solid" (gun safes, crates, chests) and let the rest
         ride on the guess. So the fix is to mark the blocks where the guess is wrong,
         not to mark everything.

         v1.213.0 CORRECTION, AND IT CHANGES WHAT THIS APPEND DOES. This paragraph used to
         say "THIS IS NOT MAKE IT A WALL... a ramp marked solid would still be walkable,
         because IsMovementBlocked reports it passable from the low side. Nothing here
         forces anything shut." There is no low side and nothing reports per side. Given
         the resolution above, and glassBulletproofRampSheet's own properties:

             Shape           New          (does not override IsMovementBlocked)
             Collide         movement,melee,bullet,arrow,rocket
             BlocksMovement  unset        (so the shape is asked, and the shape says yes)

         this append makes that block a FULLY BLOCKED CELL to the navigation grid, on
         every side. That is still the behaviour we wanted, because the fault was routes
         planned straight through the glass, and a sealed cell stops them. But it is a
         seal, not a second look, and anywhere a zombie was previously expected to walk up
         or over one of these it no longer can. Flagged rather than left to be discovered.

         The evidence for all of this is the trash can. cntDomedTrashCanCornerRandomLootHelper
         is vanilla, declares Path="solid" with Collide="movement" and BlocksMovement unset,
         exactly like the ramp above, and a knee-high bin was sealing the corner at the top
         of the Ostrich stairs so completely that routes to the cell next to it cost 31
         waypoints against 7 for the cell one block further east.

         SCOPE: glassBulletproofRampSheet ONLY. The other diagonals in the 22 Oct logs
         (plateDiagonalHalfLeft, windowRampTwoSidedBulletproof*) were blocks Fril placed
         to test this theory, not part of the build, and he said so. If the chewing moves
         to another shape, that shape joins this list and we have learned something.
         ================================================================== -->
    <append xpath="/blocks/block[@name='glassBulletproofRampSheet']">
        <property name="Path" value="solid"/>
    </append>

</configs>
